We are given $AP:PB = 1:4.$  Use the fact that $\overrightarrow{P}$ is a linear combination of $\overrightarrow{A}$  and $\overrightarrow{B}
$: \[\vec{AP} = k \overrightarrow{AB}\]for some constant $k.$  The equation that represents this is
\[(\overrightarrow{P} - \overrightarrow{A}) = k (\overrightarrow{B} - \overrightarrow{A}).\]Expand and bring like terms together to get \[\overrightarrow{P} = k \overrightarrow{B} + (k - 1) \overrightarrow{A}.\]Comparing this with $x \overrightarrow{A} + y \overrightarrow{B},$  we have $(t,u) = (k - 1,k).$</think> <answer>(4,5)</answer>

 First, notice that multiplying two matrices of this form results in
\[\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a+b \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.\] 
Because matrix multiplication is associative, we can multiply these matrices together in any order. Breaking up the product, we get \begin{align*}
& \hspace{0.2cm} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \dotsm \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \dotsm \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 31 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 33 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 35 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \dotsm \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 97 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1+3+5+\dots+97+99 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \boxed{ \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2500 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} } 
\end{align*}
